        "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-09-01/html/CREC-2026-09-01-pt1-PgH5395.htm",
        "start": 3647,
        "quote": "For nearly 30 years, the Atlantic Large Whale Take Reduction Team has \noverseen the Maine lobster and Jonah crab fisheries. They have \nimplemented and updated a take reduction plan with extensive regulatory \nrequirements designed to minimize the fishery's impact on North \nAtlantic right whales. What they are doing is working. For decades, the \nindustry has led conservation efforts to reduce right whale \nentanglements in their gear.",
        "summary": "For nearly 30 years, the Atlantic Large Whale Take Reduction Team has overseen the Maine lobster and Jonah crab fisheries. They have implemented and updated a take reduction plan with extensive regulatory requirements designed to minimize the fishery's impact on North Atlantic right whales. What they are doing is working. For decades, the industry has led conservation efforts to reduce right whale entanglements in their gear.",
        "index": 24,
        "reason": "manager-supported claim that existing conservation efforts are working and should be preserved"
      },
      {
        "bio": "W000821",
        "speaker": "Bruce Westerman",
        "side": "Yea",
        "manager": true,
        "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CREC-2026-09-01/html/CREC-2026-09-01-pt1-PgH5395.htm",
        "start": 4085,
        "quote": "Despite the American lobster fishery virtually eliminating its impact \non right whales, the take reduction plan has faced repeated litigation, \nleaving the industry subject to regulatory whiplash. The American \nlobster fishery has been subject to four different biological opinions \nbetween 2010 and 2021, with ever more draconian restrictions.",
        "summary": "Despite the American lobster fishery virtually eliminating its impact on right whales, the take reduction plan has faced repeated litigation, leaving the industry subject to regulatory whiplash. The American lobster fishery has been subject to four different biological opinions between 2010 and 2021, with ever more draconian restrictions.",
        "index": 30,
        "reason": "manager-supported need for relief from repeated litigation and regulatory whiplash"
      },
      {
        "bio": "W000821",
        "speaker": "Bruce Westerman",
